# "+ New timer" wizard with RunOnCreate=1/AutoReturnStart=1 - unlike
# create_and_start_timer.seq (which pins both to 0 and starts explicitly),
# this confirms a freshly created timer auto-starts on its own and the app
# pops back toward the watchface, without an explicit start press.
# KeyboardOnNewTimer stays 0 - see new_timer_discard_draft.seq's own note
# on why label entry isn't scripted here.
#
# NOTE: live-verified (2026-09-30) - confirmed both post-creation
# screenshots already show the system watchface (AutoReturnStart's pop
# already happened by then), so there's genuinely no app UI/timer value to
# override at either checkpoint, not just an estimation gap.
IMPORT ../common/wipe_and_prep.seq
# This timer auto-starts for real via RunOnCreate, not the
# TestSetTimerIndex backdoor - like create_and_start_timer.seq, its exact
# resulting remaining time isn't known precisely enough in advance to send
# a TestSetTimerRemainingDisplay override, so the final screenshot's timer
# row stays live (only the clock/elapsed fields are sent throughout).
APPMSG --int 10016=0 10015=1 10003=1
APPMSG --int 10033=0 --string 10031="12:00"
SCREENSHOT list_empty

TAP select
APPMSG --int 10033=0 --string 10031="12:00"
SCREENSHOT duration_dial_default
TAP up
TAP select
TAP up
TAP select
APPMSG --int 10033=0 --string 10031="12:00"
# Both remaining screenshots show the real system watchface (see header
# comment) - its own hint text rotates between messages, outside this
# app's control, so both are masked rather than tuned like an app-side
# value (see wakeup_conflict_exit_guard_reprompt.seq's identical
# checkpoint for the full reasoning and how this rect was measured).
SCREENSHOT timer_committed --mask-rect 170x45+15+93
SLEEP 1
APPMSG --int 10033=0 --string 10031="12:00"
SCREENSHOT should_already_be_running_and_near_watchface --mask-rect 170x45+15+93
